00:01:44.752 --> 00:01:45.460
But you have job?

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.210
OK, all right.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:46.720
That's good.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.860
So you're writing code, or?

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:53.270
So it's come out
of the back end.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.970
Remember Deep Blue?

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:58.600
And winning chess matches,
and Jeopardy, and--

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.520
that was all brute force.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.380
It was a dictionary-based
system that

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.080
had some heuristics
on the back side.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.750
The Jeopardy system was a
natural language-based system

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:09.340
that brute-forced
on the BI system.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.890
So at the end of the day,
those were great demo cases

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.920
to get people excited about a
machine that can look and feel

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.620
like it's thinking, or
that it's doing cognition,

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.080
or that it's doing
predictions on things.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.290
Largely, these things went
dark immediately after that.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.720
And then everyone started
working on it anyway.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.990
So pharmaceuticals,
trading applications

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:31.285
for high speed
trading, people that

00:02:31.285 --> 00:02:34.660
do protein folding modeling,
drug interactions, oil gas

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.880
field exploration,
seismic study, climate

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.210
modeling-- you get the idea.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.420
These things have
been largely the realm

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.530
of very expensive, very
boutique, and very quiet

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.730
innovation.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.100
About a year ago,
Google published

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.220
something called TensorFlow
from our Brain team, which

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.480
is an open source, deep and
wide set of frameworks that

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.090
allow you to create
your own set of systems

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:01.510
with exactly the same code
that we use internally.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:04.030
The good news there is
it's pretty well tested,

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.910
it's supported by some
wicked smart people.

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:09.460
The bad news is you're
still writing machine code.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:11.690
So what I thought
might be interesting,

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.500
and I was foolish enough to be
close enough to being correct

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.390
that they gave me
the job, was, why

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:19.670
don't we try to automate
all the tough stuff?

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.439
And leave the interesting stuff,
which normally isn't tough.

00:03:22.439 --> 00:03:24.730
How many people in the room
used to write assembly code

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:26.270
for a living?

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.284
God, and you're still
relatively sane.

00:03:28.284 --> 00:03:29.700
At least you're
all wearing pants,

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.160
which is kind of the
bar at this point.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.350
It is-- it was
really hard, right?

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.540
You kind of care how
the system worked.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.880
But the line of code you
wrote was hand-crafted.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.380
It was a thing of beauty.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.000
It worked at super
high performance,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.680
and you understood
everything about it.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.792
We're largely still at
that level for most machine

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.250
learning models
that people create.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.170
They handcraft it, they
understand it deeply,

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:50.399
or they wish they did.

00:03:50.399 --> 00:03:51.690
In the case of neural networks.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.648
And I'll explain a little
bit why that's harder

00:03:53.648 --> 00:03:55.140
than it was with wide models.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.689
But at the end of the day,
it isn't a scalable system

00:03:57.689 --> 00:03:59.730
where you can say, OK,
I'll do the initial thing,

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.920
but I have 50 people who
can probably take this

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.180
to the next level of scale.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.060
Everybody's writing that
hand-crafted level thing.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.180
So we're at the year
one, you can think of,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:14.220
having large scale access
to human producible machine

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.399
learning code, which
is quite good, not OK--

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:20.010
not a simple linear regression
model and not time series data

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.060
based stuff.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.450
So this is kind of the
beginning of the cycle

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.310
that Eric called out.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.880
And by the way, this
was from Next 16,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.380
