00:35:24.600 --> 00:35:25.940
It's not a thing.

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:29.480
A pet implies ownership
and care and feeding.

00:35:29.480 --> 00:35:32.030
But the system doesn't
mind that it's a concept.

00:35:32.030 --> 00:35:33.095
This picture is a pet.

00:35:33.095 --> 00:35:34.970
And we will largely go,
yep, that's obviously

00:35:34.970 --> 00:35:36.410
not a wild animal.

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:38.490
Mammal, animal,
British shorthair.

00:35:38.490 --> 00:35:38.990
Oh.

00:35:38.990 --> 00:35:40.240
Now we're getting interesting.

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:41.750
All the way down to close up.

00:35:41.750 --> 00:35:43.580
So now it's a meta
point about the fact

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:46.040
that the cat's head looks
relatively large, given

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:48.740
the focal distance, and there's
bokeh in the background, which

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:51.410
indicates it's probably
relatively close to the sensor

00:35:51.410 --> 00:35:52.250
image.

00:35:52.250 --> 00:35:54.660
So you get the idea that we
can do sensor-based images.

00:35:54.660 --> 00:35:56.830
Let me call out one
quick thing on here.

00:35:56.830 --> 00:36:00.150
Cat, 99% sure.

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:03.570
Vertebrate, 91% sure.

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:05.620
Wait, what?

