What we've figured out here
is it's a stadium, team sport,

00:41:03.410 --> 00:41:04.490
ballgame, baseball.

00:41:04.490 --> 00:41:06.110
All that stuff is
perfectly fine.

00:41:06.110 --> 00:41:07.330
Sports venue.

00:41:07.330 --> 00:41:09.770
OK, we're genericizing
the case a little bit.

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:11.754
Multi-sport event,
because they picked out

00:41:11.754 --> 00:41:12.920
that there's multiple lines.

00:41:12.920 --> 00:41:14.503
And it's making a
guess that maybe you

00:41:14.503 --> 00:41:15.890
can play football on this too.

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:17.900
So that's getting
pretty interesting.

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:20.570
The part I'm especially proud
of-- and it's not in the XF

00:41:20.570 --> 00:41:24.010
data for the photo--

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:27.280
is what exact baseball
stadium in the world it is.

00:41:27.280 --> 00:41:29.980
So we detect landmarks.

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:33.070
We do a pretty good job
detecting the Eiffel Tower

00:41:33.070 --> 00:41:36.880
versus the Paris
Hotel in Las Vegas

00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:40.150
versus a tricked out
picture of the Eiffel

00:41:40.150 --> 00:41:41.800
Tower held in someone's hand.

00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:43.570
We're not fooled, typically.

00:41:43.570 --> 00:41:46.356
This is a case where,
without any lat/long,

00:41:46.356 --> 00:41:48.730
we figured out what baseball
stadium in the world it was.

00:41:48.730 --> 00:41:50.290
And it's Citi Field
where the Mets play.

00:41:50.290 --> 00:41:51.831
It might be the only
chance any of us

00:41:51.831 --> 00:41:54.576
ever see it because no one goes
to a Mets game, as we can see.

00:41:54.576 --> 00:41:55.945
