00:42:30.740 --> 00:42:32.290
So you have data that comes in.

00:42:32.290 --> 00:42:33.707
You apply an
algorithm against it.

00:42:33.707 --> 00:42:35.498
The algorithm probably
isn't the right one.

00:42:35.498 --> 00:42:36.480
The data wasn't clean.

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:37.670
You don't have enough data.

00:42:37.670 --> 00:42:40.990
I'm saying this is a whole
workload that you will turn

00:42:40.990 --> 00:42:43.570
into something that you
operationalize the same way you

00:42:43.570 --> 00:42:45.347
would saying, we
have a data team,

00:42:45.347 --> 00:42:47.180
and they're pretty good
at doing data stuff.

00:42:47.180 --> 00:42:48.888
You don't say we just
have database guys.

00:42:48.888 --> 00:42:49.930
We have a data team.

00:42:49.930 --> 00:42:51.721
Some people on the team
specialize in this.

00:42:51.721 --> 00:42:53.050
Some people specialize in that.

00:42:53.050 --> 00:42:55.540
When you start operationalizing
machine learning models

00:42:55.540 --> 00:42:57.400
at scale in
production, you'll have

00:42:57.400 --> 00:42:59.470
people that specialize
in your data cleansing.

00:42:59.470 --> 00:43:00.970
You'll have some
algorithmic experts

00:43:00.970 --> 00:43:02.761
that are really good
at soft edge detection

00:43:02.761 --> 00:43:05.020
systems versus hard
edge detection systems.

00:43:05.020 --> 00:43:07.249
And yes, those are different
ends of the math branch.

00:43:07.249 --> 00:43:09.790
And then you'll discover over
time that some of these things,

00:43:09.790 --> 00:43:11.920
you know what, we don't need
to stay on the cutting edge.

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:13.919
We're just going to use
someone else's black box

00:43:13.919 --> 00:43:16.340
ones like Google's because
they're good enough.

00:43:16.340 --> 00:43:18.590
And we want to write the
specialized one where we have

00:43:18.590 --> 00:43:20.470
